* ===========================================================================
* open_mcc_and_cascade.dts -- MCC (x64) and Cascade ERP (DBF side) OPEN AT
* THE SAME TIME, in two workspaces, both live when the script ends.
*
* Run:  DOTSCRIPT D:\code\ccode\dottalkpp\data\scripts\open_mcc_and_cascade.dts
* (DOTSCRIPT wants an ABSOLUTE path from the prompt.)
*
* THIS IS A SESSION OPENER, NOT A REGRESSION. It deliberately does NOT tear
* down at the end -- leaving both workspaces open IS the deliverable. The
* teardown block at the bottom is commented out for when you are done.
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE RULE THAT MAKES THIS WORK, AND THE ONE THAT MAKES IT POSSIBLE
*
* MEMBERSHIP: an area joins whichever workspace is CURRENT when it is OPENED.
* Never open-then-assign. WORKSPACE OPEN <dir> AS <name> is itself a MINTING
* form -- it creates the workspace and opens into it in one act, so there is
* no window where a table can land in the wrong place.
*
* ADDITIVE OPEN (R128, owner 2026-08-26: "open should be additive or it will
* kill the other workspaces"): the SECOND open does not close the first. That
* ruling is the only reason this script can exist. Before it, WORKSPACE OPEN
* began with workspace_close_all() and the second call would have thrown away
* the first. TWO_T1 is the arm that proves it did not.
*
* THE INDEX SLOT IS GLOBAL, NOT PER WORKSPACE. This is the sharp edge here and
* the reason each open is preceded by its own three SET PATH lines: the two
* systems keep their indexes in different roots, and attach happens AT OPEN
* TIME against whatever the slot says then. Set the slots, open, set again,
* open again. A posture records DBFROOT/IDXROOT and can re-point itself on
* LOAD; the LIVE slots have no such per-workspace memory.
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* READ RULE -- COUNT THE MARKERS FIRST, READ THE VERDICTS SECOND.
*
*   grep -c '^? "TWO_'   <-- run this; read the run against THAT
*   Expect 5:  2 guards TWO_G0 TWO_G1, 3 arms TWO_T1 TWO_T2 TWO_T3
*
* An errored marker PRINTS NOTHING rather than going red, so a short count is
* a FAILURE wearing a clean transcript. If either TWO_G* reads .F., treat every
* TWO_T* as UNPROVEN rather than as failing -- an arm that reads an unchanged
* value cannot tell "survived" from "never worked" without its guard.
*
* Every marker is a FIELD-VALUE comparison, never a read of console text.
*
* WHY THESE ARMS SAY `GO 1` AND NOT `TOP` -- MEASURED ON THE FIRST RUN,
* 2026-08-29, AND IT IS THE MOST USEFUL THING THIS SCRIPT HAS PRODUCED.
*
* The first cut used TOP. It printed `TOP: failed.` before ALL FIVE markers
* and ALL FIVE STILL READ .T. -- because SELECT had already left each freshly
* opened table on Recno 1, which is where TOP was supposed to put it. A verb
* announced its own failure in console text no marker can read, and the arms
* were green for a reason that had nothing to do with them. That is a FALSE
* GREEN of the third shape: a real read of a real open table that the step
* under it never affected.
*
* THE CORRELATE, and it is stated as a correlate because the mechanism is NOT
* yet proven: WORKSPACE OPEN auto-attaches a container and sets NO ACTIVE TAG
* -- its scan line reads `[index: STUDENTS.cdx, attached]` and names no tag --
* and order_nav.hpp:745 answers `if (tagName.empty()) return false;`. Specs
* whose TOP works (MWXSHAKE section 5C) have no container attached at all, or
* set an order tag first, which repositions the cursor by itself.
*
* THE ONE-LINE DISCRIMINATOR, not yet run: in a live session after this
* script, `WORKSPACE SWITCH MCC` / `SELECT STUDENTS` / `SET ORDER TO TAG SID`
* / `TOP`. If TOP succeeds with a tag and fails without one, the untagged
* container is the cause. Ask the engine; do not infer it from the header.
*
* GO 1 IS THE RIGHT ADDRESSING HERE ANYWAY, tag or no tag: R121 ruled that
* ADDRESSING IS ABSOLUTE AND TRAVERSAL IS FILTERED -- GO <n> names a record
* and lands on it under any setting, while TOP names a position in a set. An
* arm that means "physical record 1 of this table reads Taylor" should say so.
*
* ===========================================================================

SET TALK ON
ECHO === OPEN MCC + CASCADE ERP INTO TWO WORKSPACES ===

* --- clean slate ---------------------------------------------------------
* CLOSE ALL first, then DESTROY: destroy REFUSES a workspace that still holds
* areas, and it does not cascade. On a first run the DESTROYs print "no such
* workspace" -- that is correct and expected, not an error.
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE DESTROY MCC
WORKSPACE DESTROY CASCERP

* --- workspace 1: MCC, the x64 teaching database -------------------------
SET PATH DBF DBF/x64
SET PATH INDEXES INDEXES/x64
SET PATH LMDB LMDB/x64
WORKSPACE OPEN DBF/x64 AS MCC

&& Expect 13 tables, NOT 14. FMGRTST.__fldbak.dbf sits in that directory and
&& is correctly skipped -- a FIELDMGR restructure leaves <stem>.__fldbak.dbf
&& beside the real table, and it used to be opened as a work area AND SORT
&& AHEAD OF IT into area 0 (AIF-133). If you ever see 14 here, that fix is gone.

WORKSPACE SWITCH MCC
SELECT STUDENTS
GO 1
? "TWO_G0_mcc_is_readable_before_cascade_opens:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "Taylor")

* --- workspace 2: Cascade ERP, the DBF side ------------------------------
* Bundle layout, owner ruling 2026-08-10: everything Cascade lives under
* systems/cascade_erp/. Slots move together or the indexes attach from the
* wrong root.
SET PATH DBF SYSTEMS/CASCADE_ERP/DBF
SET PATH INDEXES SYSTEMS/CASCADE_ERP/INDEXES
SET PATH LMDB SYSTEMS/CASCADE_ERP/LMDB
WORKSPACE OPEN SYSTEMS/CASCADE_ERP/DBF AS CASCERP

&& Expect 43 tables. AS CASCERP is doing real work: the directory leaf here is
&& "DBF", so without it this workspace would be named DBF.

WORKSPACE SWITCH CASCERP
SELECT CASCADE_Sales_Orders
GO 1
? "TWO_G1_cascade_is_readable_when_it_opens:" + (SO_ID = 1)

* --- both open at once: the registry, then the arms ----------------------
WORKSPACE REGISTRY

&& TWO_T1 IS THE DISCRIMINATOR AND IT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE SCRIPT.
&& Go back to MCC AFTER Cascade opened and read the same row TWO_G0 read.
&& Under a replacing OPEN, MCC's 13 areas are gone and this reds. Under the
&& additive OPEN it reads Taylor again.
WORKSPACE SWITCH MCC
SELECT STUDENTS
GO 1
? "TWO_T1_mcc_survived_the_second_open:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "Taylor")

&& TWO_T2: and the traffic goes both ways -- Cascade is still there after
&& switching away from it and back. A one-directional check would pass on an
&& implementation that quietly kept only the workspace you asked for last.
WORKSPACE SWITCH CASCERP
SELECT CASCADE_Sales_Orders
GO 1
? "TWO_T2_cascade_survived_the_switch_away:" + (SO_ID = 1)

&& TWO_T3: a DIFFERENT table in the second workspace, so the arm is not just
&& re-reading the one cursor that happened to be parked. Items is the
&& inventory hub, the most-referenced parent in the bundle.
SELECT CASCADE_Items
GO 1
? "TWO_T3_a_second_cascade_table_is_live_too:" + (ITEM_ID >= 1)

* --- where you are now ---------------------------------------------------
ECHO
ECHO === BOTH WORKSPACES ARE OPEN -- READ THIS BEFORE YOU SWITCH ===
ECHO
ECHO   WORKSPACE SWITCH MOVES MEMBERSHIP. IT DOES NOT MOVE THE PATH SLOTS.
ECHO   Both systems stay open and readable either way, but anything that has
ECHO   to RESOLVE a container or an LMDB env goes through the GLOBAL slot,
ECHO   which belongs to whichever workspace was opened last. So a switch is
ECHO   FOUR LINES, not one. Measured 2026-08-29: SET ORDER TO TAG SID on
ECHO   MCC's STUDENTS answered
ECHO     "openCdx: LMDB env missing: ...SYSTEMS\CASCADE_ERP\LMDB\STUDENTS.cdx.d"
ECHO   -- an MCC table looked up under the Cascade bundle.
ECHO
ECHO   TO WORK IN MCC:
ECHO     WORKSPACE SWITCH MCC
ECHO     SET PATH DBF DBF/x64
ECHO     SET PATH INDEXES INDEXES/x64
ECHO     SET PATH LMDB LMDB/x64
ECHO
ECHO   TO WORK IN CASCADE:
ECHO     WORKSPACE SWITCH CASCERP
ECHO     SET PATH DBF SYSTEMS/CASCADE_ERP/DBF
ECHO     SET PATH INDEXES SYSTEMS/CASCADE_ERP/INDEXES
ECHO     SET PATH LMDB SYSTEMS/CASCADE_ERP/LMDB
ECHO
ECHO   WORKSPACE REGISTRY  -- who owns which engine slot
ECHO   Address tables BY NAME after a switch, never by slot number.
ECHO   The slots are left pointing at the CASCADE bundle by this script.
ECHO

* ===========================================================================
* NOT COVERED, stated rather than implied:
*
*   - THAT A SCOPED CLOSE OF ONE SPARES THE OTHER. It does (RELSCOPE2 and
*     WSMULTI both assert it), but closing is the opposite of this script's
*     job, so nothing here reads it.
*   - NAME COLLISION ACROSS THE TWO. These two systems share no table name,
*     so SELECT <name> is unambiguous today. If that ever changes, know that
*     SELECT resolves UNSCOPED and returns the LOWEST engine slot with no
*     diagnostic (R112 first-wins; AIF-137 fixed the relation paths, not
*     SELECT). Qualify by switching workspaces first, which this script does.
*   - THE 13 AND THE 43. Console counts, which no marker can read; verify
*     them in the transcript (the IDXDIFF precedent).
*   - THAT EITHER WORKSPACE CAN DO INDEX WORK. It cannot, not without moving
*     the slots first. NOTHING HERE ASSERTS THAT, and it is the sharpest
*     limitation of running two systems at once: WORKSPACE SWITCH is scoped,
*     SET PATH is not. A posture records DBFROOT/IDXROOT and re-points itself
*     on LOAD; the LIVE slots have no per-workspace memory at all. An arm for
*     it would need a field-value read AFTER a SET ORDER in each workspace,
*     which is a spec of its own and is not this script's job.
*
* IT ADOPTS, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. WORKSPACE OPEN ... AS prints "ADOPTED the
* durable identity this name already holds in the catalog" on every run after
* the first, because the opening DESTROYs only reach workspaces DECLARED IN
* THIS SESSION and a fresh process has none. For a spec that would be
* history-dependence wearing a green suit; for a session opener it is the
* point -- MCC means the same durable workspace tomorrow that it means today.
* CORRECTED 2026-08-29 BY READING THE CATALOG: this line used to end "Each
* run still writes a birth row to the catalog either way", and that is FALSE.
* ADOPTION DOES NOT MINT. Two runs of this script left exactly ONE MCC row and
* ONE CASCERP row (both stamped 06:37:13, the first run); the second run
* printed ADOPTED and added nothing. So the durable cost of this script is TWO
* rows ONCE, not two rows per run -- which is the opposite of what a regression
* spec pays, and the reason adoption is right here and wrong there. Measured by
* reading WORKSPACE CATALOG, not by reasoning about the verb.
*
* TEARDOWN, when you are done with the session. Uncomment to use:
*
* WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
* WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
* WORKSPACE DESTROY CASCERP
* WORKSPACE DESTROY MCC
* ===========================================================================
